<DocsLayout title="Team sync in Haft v9" description="Use git-tracked markdown as an exchange carrier and explicit sync into each engineer's local SQLite runtime store.">
  <h1>Team sync</h1>

  <p>
    Haft is local-first. Each engineer has a project SQLite store under
    <code>~/.haft/projects/&lt;project-id&gt;/</code>. Reviewable
    <code>.haft/*.md</code> projections travel through git.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>One distinction matters:</strong> SQLite is the local runtime
      structured store. Markdown is the publication and exchange carrier. The
      file transports a record; it does not act, validate itself, or become
      another engineer's runtime state until explicit sync.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Exchange workflow</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Engineer A creates or updates a Haft record through its effect sink.</li>
    <li>Haft writes the corresponding <code>.haft/*.md</code> carrier.</li>
    <li>A reviews and commits the carrier with the related code.</li>
    <li>Engineer B pulls the branch.</li>
    <li>B runs <code>haft sync</code>.</li>
    <li>B's local SQLite store imports the supported carrier data.</li>
  </ol>

  <pre><code>git pull
haft sync
haft check</code></pre>

  <p>
    Sync is explicit and idempotent for the same supported input. It is not a
    background replication protocol. Schema mismatch or malformed structured
    data must remain visible; do not coerce a partial carrier into current
    semantics.
  </p>

  <h2>What the carrier preserves</h2>

  <p>
    Current artifact projections include a hidden structured-data block so the
    supported typed payload can round-trip into SQLite. Older carriers without
    that envelope may be imported as legacy with audit warnings. Missing source
    meaning remains unresolved rather than being upgraded by guesswork.
  </p>

  <h2>Review in the same change</h2>

  <p>
    Include the <code>.haft/</code> diff in the pull request that changes the
    governed code. Reviewers can inspect the readable rationale, scope,
    invariants, evidence coordinates, and lifecycle metadata next to the
    implementation.
  </p>

  <pre><code>git diff main...feature-branch -- .haft/</code></pre>

  <p>
    The presence of a carrier in a pull request is not proof that the code
    implements it or that its evidence is current. Use code context, spec
    checks, and verification for those separate questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Specification carriers</h2>

  <p>
    Typed SpecSections use their own lifecycle and SQL edition store. Use
    <code>haft spec sync</code>, <code>haft spec validate</code>, and
    <code>haft spec check</code> for specification carriers. Do not infer
    lifecycle approval from a successful git merge or generic artifact sync.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>There is no real-time shared database in the v9 product.</li>
    <li>Git carries file conflicts; Haft does not silently pick one semantic winner.</li>
    <li>Each engineer's code index and runtime state can have different freshness until refreshed and synced.</li>
    <li>Authority provenance travels with the record; importing a carrier does not create a new operator approval.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Do not hand-edit lifecycle or authority fields.</strong> Use the
      public effect sink. A readable markdown carrier is not a bypass around
      kernel validation or human gates.
    </p>
  </div>

